<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jubba River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Juba River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jubba is a river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, formed by the Ganale and Dawa rivers that rise in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian Highlands</a>, which flows south across Somalia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> near Kismayo. With the Shabelle it waters the fertile inter-riverine region, home to farming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> clans and the Ajuran Sultanate, and it gave its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jubaland">Jubaland</a>, ceded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a> in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jubba-river.jpg" alt="Jubba River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United Nations Assistance Mission in Somalia (UNSOM) / UN Photo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jubba_River_in_Goobweyn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
